       incline at an angle measuring 60 degrees or more." The ground rode every incline alike, so a body walked up the
       mesa's bare face and slid down it, and a fall onto it stopped halfway. Now, for her and every body:
         - feet do not carry a body UP a wall (it is climbed, a ladder or a rope, or not at all);
         - a body on a wall is not standing: it falls down the face, and lands at its foot, the whole height priced;
         - a deck is never a wall (its planks are flat whatever the ground under them does).
